  "query_preview": "..The weak upper low that moved through eastern AZ\nTue/Wed has exited the region this morning. The strong area of\nhigh pressure centered over the southern plains will expand and\nshift westward over the next few days, with 500 mb heights\ngenerally 596-598 dam over our region through at least Sunday\nwhich is well above the median values for late July. Monsoon\nmoisture levels will decrease somewhat but still remain elevated \nenough for isolated to scattered afternoon and evening showers and\nthunderstorms today through Saturday. Daytime temperatures look \nto peak Friday and Saturday at 5-10 degrees above normal. By \nSunday through Tuesday - the center of the high is forecast to \nshift back east a bit and allow for an increase in monsoon \nmoisture from the southeast into our area. Daily rain chances go \nback up to 40-60% for higher terrain and 20-30% for lower \nelevations. This may also result in temperatures backing off for \nall but far northern Arizona. We issued an Extreme Heat Warning \nfor the lower elevations of the Grand Canyon and Marble/Glen \nCanyons from Friday through Sunday where highs may reach as high \nas 112-114 near the Colorado River. Lower elevations of Yavapai \nCounty also look to be rather hot through this period, though \nconfidence in extreme heat is lower there due to moisture/clouds \npossibly keeping temperatures from reaching their potential with \nsuch a strong ridge in place. We will continue to monitor the \ntemperature forecast in this area to evaluate the need for a heat \nwarning there also.",
